'''Hawford''' is a hamlet in [[Worcestershire]], England, located in the outer suburbs of [[Worcester, England|Worcester]], about {{convert|3|mi}} from the city centre.

It falls within two parishes, [[North Claines]] and [[Ombersley]]. It is bisected by the [[River Salwarpe]] and the Droitwich-Worcester Canal, and is also bounded by the [[River Severn]].

==Landmarks==
Hawford Dovecote, owned by the [[National Trust]], is a 16th-century half-timbered building. It is a remnant of a former monastic grange.<ref>{{NHLE|desc=Hawford Dovecote |num=1081151|grade=II*|access-date=7 November 2025}}</ref><ref>{{NHLE|desc=Dovecote at Hawford Grange |num=1005261 |grade=<!--Not applicable to this entry-->|access-date=7 November 2025}}</ref> The Trust describes it as "virtually unaltered since the late 16th century".<ref>National Trust, [https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/visit/worcestershire-herefordshire/hawford-dovecote Hawford Dovecote], accessed on 26 February 2026</ref>

{{anchor|House}}'''Hawford House''', located one mile to the south of Hawford,<ref>[[Google Maps]]</ref> is a Grade II [[listed building|listed]] 18th century house, now converted to apartments.<ref>{{NHLE|desc=Hawford House |num=1173079 |grade=II|access-date=7 November 2025}}</ref> Charles Castle, of Hawford House, served as [[High Sheriff of Worcestershire]] in 1881.

